00:00:06.155 --> 00:00:07.280
CARTER PAGE: Hi, everybody.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.355
Welcome to our session.

00:00:08.355 --> 00:00:09.730
It's the first
time I've done one

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.710
of these in a movie theater.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.600
And the seats look
very comfortable.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.610
So try not to fall asleep.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.845
But if you do, there
is a full recline mode

00:00:18.845 --> 00:00:20.470
apparently on one of
the buttons there.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.960
So I see a couple have already
taken advantage of this.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:24.460
OK.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.010
So we're going to be talking
about building a Global Data

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:30.310
Presence with Bigtable--
that's a title.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:31.660
And my name is Carter Page.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.480
I'm the engineering lead for
Cloud Bigtable at Google.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.050
And today joining us is going
to be Subhasree Mandal, who

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.810
is a principal engineer
in the networking group,

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:44.120
and Doug Mcerlean, who was
an early architect of Cloud

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:44.620
Bigtable.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.315
And he will be our
DJ during the demos.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.450
So we're talking about
moving communication--

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.040
moving data around the world.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.710
And moving data quickly--
low-latency access to data

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.620
has always been an
important and valuable thing

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.710
to leaders in business and
governments, et cetera.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:06.790
You go back to 490 BC, you
have Pheidippides running

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:10.630
the distance of a marathon to
Athens and in his last dying

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.363
breath, announces victory
over the Persians.

00:01:13.363 --> 00:01:16.310
You fast forward just
another 2,000 years.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.760
And you have victory over
Napoleon at Waterloo.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.400
And the Rothschild
family at this time

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.970
had a famous communication
network throughout Europe.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:30.260
Their families spread around
the continent and in England.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:33.650
And using horses
and people and maybe

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.690
carrier pigeons at
this point in time,

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:39.050
they transmitted information
of the defeat of Napoleon

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:43.130
a full 48 hours before the
prime minister's own messengers

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.780
got word to them, allowing them
to make a run on British bonds

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.390
and increase their already
massive fortune in a very

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.510
famous case of
information arbitrage.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.340
Just 200 years
later and now you're

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.360
able to transmit entire copies
of the Library of Congress

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.520
around the world
in just minutes.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.490
And it's a truly amazing
accomplishment for mankind

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.480
if you think about it.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.220
Even just 20 years ago,
if you wanted to do this,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.220
you'd be better served
by throwing digital tapes

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.140
in the back of a FedEx truck.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.350
So now you can move this
data around really quickly.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:18.800
But the hard part is actually
keeping actual data corpuses

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.623
in sync on opposite
sides of the planet.

00:02:20.623 --> 00:02:22.040
And that's a little
bit what we're

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.910
going to talk about today.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.210
So we put out a blog
post this morning.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.370
We announced that
we actually went GA

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:31.970
with the global replication
for Cloud Bigtable.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:33.483
And what this will
allow you to do--

00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:35.150
and we'll go into
more detail in a bit--

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.430
is it will allow
you to replicate

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:40.650
your data between any four
regions around the world.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:44.660
So you can have them
two zones in one region,

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.020
two zones in another region.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.030
Or you could have one zone
in each of four regions.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.915
So this gives you data look--

00:02:52.915 --> 00:02:54.790
actually let me go back
to this for a second.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.053
So in addition to the
immediate access--

00:02:58.053 --> 00:02:59.720
so one of the things
I want to point out

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.200
is this basically gives you
low latency access for each

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.130
of the different data points.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.490
So this means a smoother
experience for your customers.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:10.960
Imagine you have your
customers in one country or one

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:11.575
continent.

00:03:11.575 --> 00:03:14.200
And maybe you have call centers
on the other side of the world.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.700
If they both have low-latency
access to the same data,

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.575
it's going to be a
better experience.

00:03:17.575 --> 00:03:19.540
It's going to be less
lag time when people

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:20.650
are calling on the phone.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.192
There's a bunch of
other applications

00:03:22.192 --> 00:03:23.840
we'll get into, as well.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.870
Another value here
is unified analysis.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.350
Imagine you're collecting data
from a bunch of different sites

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:31.150
or users around the world.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.700
If you don't replicate
into a single place,

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.940
then you have to run ETL jobs
as something to pull it in.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.340
This just gets your
data within minutes--

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.100
usually within seconds, in
fact-- to a single place.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.160
And you can run your analytics
workloads in that location.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.980
Pick one of your zones to do
for that type of workload.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.320
And the last thing you can
do is you basically get

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.930
an out-of-the-box disaster
recovery solution.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:56.110
So you spin up
another zone that just

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:59.950
sits there to be ready in
case you have a problem.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.290
And we can either
automatically failover

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:05.590
or you can failover
manually yourself.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.398
And then you get
data localization.

00:04:07.398 --> 00:04:09.565
You specify exactly where
you want the data to live.

00:04:09.565 --> 00:04:13.570
And this is obviously important
for finance and other sorts

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:16.690
of industries that have strict
jurisdictional restrictions

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:20.190
in terms of where the data sits.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.552
So today I'm going to talk about
what Cloud Bigtable is and get

00:04:23.552 --> 00:04:25.260
into the details--
the global replication

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.648
and how you can use it.

00:04:26.648 --> 00:04:28.440
Then Subhasree is going
to come up and talk

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.820
about our global cloud network--

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.122
the fiber that spans the globe.

00:04:33.122 --> 00:04:35.580
You probably heard about this
a little bit in our keynotes.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.497
She's also going to get
into the software that

00:04:37.497 --> 00:04:39.100
helps to make it work.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.380
And then Doug is going to
give us a demonstration

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.880
just to show how easy it is to
actually set up a global data

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.650
presence on the stage.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.340
So and just so
we're not cheating

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.030
by using like a
small amount of data,

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.210
we're going to start
with 10 terabytes--

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:55.740
12-- sorry, 12 terabytes.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.915
And it's not instantaneous.

00:04:57.915 --> 00:05:00.100
It does take some
minutes to start copying.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:03.100
So we're going to start with
Doug and get that copy set up.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.890
And then we'll come back to
it later in the presentation.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:11.900
DOUG MCERLEAN: Hey, so I'm Doug.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:14.270
I'm one of the engineers
on the Cloud Bigtable team.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.040
And I'm one of the developers
of the replication feature.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.358
And Carter just painted a
pretty cool picture, right?

00:05:20.358 --> 00:05:21.650
You can put your data anywhere.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:22.900
We'll keep it in sync for you.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.800
You don't have to do
anything except click

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.330
a couple of buttons.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.640
I'm sure some of you are
wondering how true that really

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:29.140
is, right?

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.370
What are the asterisks
on this announcement?

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.070
Do you have to change
your apps that are running

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:36.620
to be safe with replication?

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.600
Do you need to stop your traffic
while you're setting it up?

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.800
Do you need to lock in your
topology first and then copy

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.920
data into it yourself?

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.027
And can you really put
data anywhere you want?

00:05:49.027 --> 00:05:50.360
Or are there restrictions there?

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:57.380
And it turns out the answer
to all these questions is no.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.210
You don't have any
of these asterisks.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.300
We will take care of all
of these things for you.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.640
And I am going to
hopefully show you

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.860
how easy this is live on stage.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.082
This is a terrifying
demo to give.

00:06:10.082 --> 00:06:11.540
But so like Carter
mentioned, we've

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.550
got 12 Ts sitting
in Oregon right now.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.605
And I am going to
click a couple buttons.

00:06:16.605 --> 00:06:18.980
And we're going to copy it--
if we want to go to the next

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:20.060
slide--

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.910
to South Carolina,
Finland, and Taiwan.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.890
And with any luck,
it's going to be

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:28.530
done by the time I come
back up in 20 or 30 minutes.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.160
So in order to get this
started, we're just

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:34.330
going to go over to--
can we get the demo up?

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.592
We go over to the
Edit Instance page.

00:06:36.592 --> 00:06:39.050
And I want to emphasize this
is the Edit Instance page, not

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:40.400
the Create Instance page.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.450
You're able to change
this whenever you want.

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:47.040
And all you have to do is add
clusters to your instance.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.490
Every cluster is a set
of nodes in a zone.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:53.055
And every cluster in your
instance serves every table.

00:06:53.055 --> 00:06:55.430
And if you change your mind
later, deletes are also safe.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:57.980
We'll hold that data
behind the scenes for you

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.410
until it's safely copied out.

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:02.650
So I'm just going to add
a couple clusters here.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.790
All right, first, US East 1.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.430
That's South Carolina.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:10.250
And then we're going
to add Europe North

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:14.480
1, which is Finland.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.720
And then we're going to add
Asia East 1, which is Taiwan.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.130
And then I'm just
going to hit Save.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:25.220
And now we're done, right?

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:25.720
I can leave.

00:07:28.533 --> 00:07:30.450
So this is going to spin
for a couple seconds.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.700
Then we're going to show you
what's actually going on.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.900
All right, so we're back
on the Instance Overview.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.473
And we see already we've
got four clusters here.

00:07:43.473 --> 00:07:44.890
We've got some
spinners over here.

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:46.810
A couple of them are
already finished.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.280
We're provisioning nodes.

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:51.080
And what this means is as
soon as this spinner is done,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.520
you can create a new table
and start using it right away.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.040
These clusters are happy
to do that for you.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.680
Over on the right, we
see some other spinners.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.567
Those are for your
existing tables, which

00:08:01.567 --> 00:08:02.650
have started copying over.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.780
And that's what I'm going
to come back up and show,

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.102
hopefully, finished
in a little bit.

00:08:07.102 --> 00:08:08.560
But we can see some
of the progress

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.202
there by going over to
the tables list page.

00:08:11.202 --> 00:08:12.910
All right, so this
says we've got the one

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:14.060
table with all this data.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:15.220
And it's copying.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:18.927
And you can expand this to
show its state in each cluster.

00:08:18.927 --> 00:08:20.510
It's copying in all
of them right now.

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:21.760
These are going to turn
into progress bars.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:22.677
There's one right now.

00:08:22.677 --> 00:08:24.158
It's at 1%.

00:08:24.158 --> 00:08:26.200
All of these are eventually
going to start going.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.450
And hopefully they should
all be green later on.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:32.650
But another thing
that I mentioned

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:35.380
is do you have to stop your
traffic while this is going on?

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:37.390
Is it unsafe to keep writing?

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:38.770
And the answer is no.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:44.620
And to show that, I've actually
got a loop running already

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:48.650
which is writing victory into
row Athens in this table.

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:50.140
And you can see that here.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.510
All of this is going to
the original cluster.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:55.990
And even as these new
clusters come online--

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:57.640
if we go back to the slide--

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:03.240
we've got-- and can
we go to the next one?

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:04.937
We've got default routing.

00:09:04.937 --> 00:09:06.020
This comes out of the box.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:07.270
You don't have to do anything.

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:09.220
If you don't tell
us what to do, we

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.180
are going to keep
your traffic flowing

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:15.440
as it always has to the original
cluster in your instance.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:17.600
And this is so that
you don't have to make

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:19.400
any changes to set this up.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.380
All your existing
apps keep working.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.630
And these new copies are
essentially backups until you

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.070
tell us otherwise.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.430
So all these writes are going
into the original cluster.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:30.840
And just to show there's
nothing up my sleeve,

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.870
I'm going to show you
the command that I ran.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:36.920
This is a simple loop
over the CPT command line.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:42.470
It's doing a set for
demo table, row Athens,

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:45.992
the demo column
family equals victory.

00:09:45.992 --> 00:09:47.700
So I'm going to start
running that again.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.283
And that's going to
go the whole time.

00:09:49.283 --> 00:09:50.900
And then when I come
back, we're going

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:52.610
to count how many
times this wrote

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.690
into the original cluster.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:56.690
And we're going to count
how many times it wrote

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.460
into one of the new copies.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.398
And they should be the
same, because we're

00:10:00.398 --> 00:10:01.440
keeping the data in sync.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.688
And so with that, I'm going
to hand things back to Carter.

00:10:04.688 --> 00:10:05.480
CARTER PAGE: Hello?

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.380
All right, thanks, Doug.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:08.630
OK, so we got that kicked off.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.640
We'll come back later
and do some fun things

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:14.918
with this new global
instance we have set up.

00:10:14.918 --> 00:10:17.210
All right, so I'm going to
talk a little bit about what

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:18.980
is Cloud Bigtable,
and how does it work,

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:21.170
and what do you
actually use it for?

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:25.570
So first let me get into
the replication thing

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:26.820
that we're talking about here.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:30.170
So a year ago, we announced
a regional replication

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:33.620
which allowed you to
put multiple clusters

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:34.640
into a single region.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:37.040
So it used to be
a zonal service.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:39.080
And now you could
have two zones,

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:42.415
two clusters running in separate
zones in a single region.

00:10:42.415 --> 00:10:43.790
And this gave you
a few benefits.

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:47.180
It gave you an extra 9 on your
SLA by expanding your failure

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:48.410
domains.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:51.925
It allowed users to isolate
serve and analytics workloads.

00:10:51.925 --> 00:10:53.300
And some presenters
have actually

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:54.770
talked about how they do that.

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:56.690
Spotify does this
a bit where you

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:59.480
can, say, have one cluster
that is serving low latency

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:00.980
traffic to your customers.

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:02.647
And you have another
cluster that you're

00:11:02.647 --> 00:11:06.020
pounding with a
MapReduce or Dataflow job

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:08.330
and just beating
it up, essentially.

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:10.070
You can have isolation
so they're not

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:11.772
interfering with each other.

00:11:11.772 --> 00:11:13.730
To that point, you can
also independently scale

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:14.340
the clusters.

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:17.300
So you could scale
up your batch cluster

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.410
to make that go faster
while that's happening,

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:22.970
and keep your serve cluster
at a different level.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:26.510
And it also provides, if
you're using the application

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:29.630
profile that supports
this, then you

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:30.860
would get automatic failover.

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:32.610
So if one zone failed,
all of your traffic

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:34.940
would automatically
go to the other zone.

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.810
So today what we're announcing
is the GA global replication.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:40.610
And there's two main
changes with this.

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.370
This allows you, one,
you get to replicate

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.170
between four zones, not two.

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:47.510
And it completely removes the
restriction on what region

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:48.860
those zones are in.

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:51.788
So, as I mentioned
before, you could put one

00:11:51.788 --> 00:11:53.330
on the East Coast,
or the West Coast.

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:55.580
You could put two--

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:58.440
you could have a
Japanese cluster.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.440
You could have one in
Osaka, one in Tokyo.

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:02.827
And then you can also fan
these out around the globe.

00:12:02.827 --> 00:12:04.910
And I'll talk a little bit
about why you might use

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:07.520
some of these different things.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.280
So Bigtable itself--
the main reasons

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:13.800
to use Bigtable is
speed and low latency.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:18.140
So it's a very high
throughput system.

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:21.780
And this throughput is
nice and linearly scalable.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:24.170
We did a presentation
a couple of years

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:27.620
ago, in fact, where
we demonstrated

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:29.240
that Bigtable could handle--

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:30.650
we threw 3,000 nodes up.

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:33.860
And we were able to get 30
million QPS durable writes

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:36.080
and 30 gigabytes per second.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.540
And that was far from the
peak of what we could actually

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:41.310
pull off on the system.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:43.070
The other thing
that you get while--

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:44.570
this is kind of the
remarkable thing

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:47.267
is there's other ways you can
do very high speed ingestion.

00:12:47.267 --> 00:12:49.100
But the trick about
Bigtable is while you're

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:53.960
blasting this data in, the split
second, or split nanosecond,

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:57.800
after we acknowledge your write,
it is immediately accessible

00:12:57.800 --> 00:12:58.460
for reads.

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:01.020
And the read
latency is very low,

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:03.352
so single digit
millisecond latency.

00:13:03.352 --> 00:13:05.810
So if you're using flash, maybe
three to four milliseconds,

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:08.600
HDD maybe a couple
of milliseconds more.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.100
But generally, the
long tail itself

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:12.450
stays within about
10 milliseconds.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:15.410
And that's a nice set
of characteristics.

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:17.910
And then with this you get
a very linear scalability.

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:20.990
So the knob use on Bigtable--
the only knob we give you

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.160
for configuration is nodes.

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:26.750
So you start with three as
your basic production cluster.

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:30.420
And then you can scale it up
from three, to 10, to 300.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:33.020
And as mentioned, we've gone
as high as 3,000 before.

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:35.300
And the thing that's kind
of nifty about Bigtable

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:40.790
is the scalability curve is
actually linear well beyond--

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:44.720
from 30 at least to
the 30 million point.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:48.020
And that's pretty nifty,
because usually these curves

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:51.050
start to flatten out a
little bit over time.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:53.480
Internally, we've optimized
the bottlenecks enough

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:57.410
where it goes linear way beyond
these bounds, essentially.

00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:00.130
And this is valuable
for planning,

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:02.380
in terms of thinking about
how many nodes you're going

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:03.588
to need for a different load.

00:14:03.588 --> 00:14:05.690
You don't have to test it out.

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:09.110
Once you get certain sample
loads that are representative,

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:10.850
you know, get a
good sense of what

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:13.740
size cluster you
would need to handle,

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.530
say 10x that, 100x that.

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:17.120
It's also good for
business purposes,

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:19.160
because if your
business is attached

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:21.800
to the amount of
operations that you're

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:23.300
having on your
Bigtable, then you

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:24.750
could predict cost of revenue.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:27.540
And that's useful.

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:29.850
So part of the way
that Bigtable does this

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:31.470
is that abstracts
out the storage

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:33.420
from the actual
processing layer.

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.940
So your Bigtable nodes
are essentially--

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:38.070
they're kind of quasi
stateless things

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:41.190
that sit on top of the actual
storage system, which is a file

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:45.150
system called Colossus, which is
a newer generation system that

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:47.550
replaced our earlier
Google File System.

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.860
And the nodes are
responsible for making sure

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.440
that the data is there
to be read quickly.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:54.530
So it's holding onto
the indexes in memory.

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:56.280
And it's also channeling
your data through

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:59.203
to make sure you're getting that
really nice write throughput.

00:14:59.203 --> 00:15:01.620
But by keeping them separate,
you can do some cool things.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:05.560
Like we can do really aggressive
re-balancing, which we do.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:10.200
So imagine the A
over there represents

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:13.890
a single chunk of data.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:17.050
And this chunk of data is
getting a lot of activity.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:20.550
Maybe it's a pattern that
crops up every day at 2:30

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:21.780
in the afternoon.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.730
And what Bigtable
will do is we'll

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:25.110
say, OK, so A is getting hot.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.810
So I'm going to start moving
things off of this node

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:32.880
so this node can serve more
of the A traffic, essentially.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.590
And Bigtable does
this fairly quickly.

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:37.050
It checks these patterns
every couple of minutes

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:38.520
or so and just
keeps re-balancing.

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.770
It does this all day, every day.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:43.920
So even as patterns
change throughout the day,

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.980
Bigtable will come back and keep
readjusting the same patterns

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:50.850
as it sees them day in, day out.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:54.660
Another advantage is
you get very, very easy

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:56.400
resizing, very fast resizing.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:59.820
So if you're familiar with
other systems like Cassandra

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:02.880
and things like that where you
have to deal with re-indexing,

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:05.820
resizing is kind of a pain
in a lot of those systems.

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:08.390
Here, you can just
throw nodes at it.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:10.530
The nodes will be
available in seconds.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:12.450
It'll take several
minutes for us

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:17.340
to re-balance to get the
maximum bin packing of your load

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:19.320
onto these nodes.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.460
And then you can do some
cool things with this.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:24.420
So one is it's good for
operational issues--

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:26.625
surprise traffic, just
throw some nodes at it.

00:16:26.625 --> 00:16:28.500
Another could be at the
end of the week maybe

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:31.230
you have a large batch
process which takes 10 hours.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:33.570
And you wish it
would take one hour.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:37.530
So you just up your cluster
by 10x, run your batch load,

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:39.000
and then spin it
back down again.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.030
OK, so here's a few
different use cases.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:45.080
So one is Ad tech.

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:51.040
So Google has a little bit of
Ad tech inside our company.

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:54.310
And we do a lot of this
stuff on top of Bigtable.

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:56.830
It's a low latency
system, which is nice.

00:16:56.830 --> 00:17:00.850
And what you can do is you can
use Google Replication to put

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:03.380
your data where your users are.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:05.710
So Bigtable's already low
latency, as I mentioned.

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:07.839
But then if you can
actually put your data

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:09.790
even closer to your
customers, then that

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:12.770
can shave off milliseconds
of network traffic.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:14.660
So in this example,
we have a zone

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:19.460
set up on the east coast, the
west coast, and central US.

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:21.240
Another example is
financial services.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:23.150
And this gets to the
disaster recovery

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:24.733
situation I was talking about.

00:17:24.733 --> 00:17:26.150
You could set up
a couple of zones

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:29.780
to be your primary high
availability service--

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:31.400
pretty close to Wall Street.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:32.930
You're not going to do high
frequency trading on this,

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:35.430
but it's good enough for back
office operations and things

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:35.930
like that.

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:38.400
It's still going to be very
fast and very high throughput.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.358
And then we've got a
South Carolina zone that's

00:17:40.358 --> 00:17:42.440
about 400 miles to the south.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:44.750
And so you could set that up.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:47.240
And we will just be
replicating your data there.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:48.620
If you have a problem--

00:17:48.620 --> 00:17:51.250
you know, if Norfolk,
Virginia goes down,

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:53.870
if there's a
hurricane or whatever,

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:56.240
then we will automatically
failover to South Carolina.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:58.430
Or if you prefer, we could
have a manual failover

00:17:58.430 --> 00:17:59.840
to South Carolina.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:02.210
And your recovery
point objective

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:04.790
is going to be measured
in minutes, again,

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:05.768
in the worst case--

00:18:05.768 --> 00:18:08.060
that would be the long tail
of the replication latency.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:10.070
Most of your data will
be there in just seconds.

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:11.780
And your replication
time objective,

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:13.990
if you're doing
automatic failover,

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:17.150
can be measured in seconds.

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:19.850
The third case I'll mention
and the third case guess

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:22.285
I'll go into detail on is IoT.

00:18:22.285 --> 00:18:23.660
So we have a lot
of customers who

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:25.490
use us for telemetry storage.

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:27.530
There's actually a lot
of industrial IoT cases.

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:30.380
There's a cool company
called [INAUDIBLE],,

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:33.920
which uses us for oil
platforms and shipping.

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.070
Go check them out
on the expo floor.

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:38.390
It's pretty neat.

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:40.700
In this case, I'm giving an
example of commercial IoT,

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:43.820
where imagine you have a
mobile phone app, maybe,

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:46.968
that collects data
on exercise habits.

00:18:46.968 --> 00:18:49.010
And you want to have a
conversation, essentially,

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:49.880
with your customer.

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:52.190
You're getting telemetry
data from the customer.

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:55.130
You want to process that
data, give them advice back.

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:57.830
If you just have
your data in the US,

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:00.950
then you're going to have a poor
experience for everybody else.

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:03.290
In this case, you could
throw one cluster in each

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:04.930
of your four different--

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:07.515
in four different continents,
and be close to customers

00:19:07.515 --> 00:19:09.140
all around the world,
essentially-- low

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.300
latency everywhere.

00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:13.940
And then you could
have your data--

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:16.670
you could do your analytics
in a single place.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:19.650
So imagine you make your Iowa
cluster a little bit bigger.

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:22.740
And that's getting all the
data from all of the other four

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:23.240
continents.

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:27.110
And then you're running maybe
longer daily batch processing

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:30.250
on that data, as well.

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:32.310
So other use cases for
Cloud Bigtable-- we

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:33.840
have gaming customers.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:37.050
Again, the low
latency there is nice,

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:39.960
being able to get a lot of
data from a lot of users

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:42.360
that high write
throughput is useful.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:43.380
It's a big data system.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:47.490
So genome sequencing is
another use case for Bigtable.

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:49.410
And then energy,
utilities, and telecom all

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:52.350
have variations of
things that work well

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:54.580
for Bigtable--
geospatial data, again,

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:56.670
things that often look
like the IoT use cases

00:19:56.670 --> 00:20:00.490
I was talking about
before, as well.

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:02.490
But to make all this work,
you have the software

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:03.490
that has Cloud Bigtable.

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:06.713
But we're kind of
glossing over the fact

00:20:06.713 --> 00:20:08.130
that the data has
to actually move

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:09.540
between these different places.

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:13.705
And that's actually
really the hard part.

00:20:13.705 --> 00:20:15.330
And you need to make
this network work.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:16.113
It has to scale.

00:20:16.113 --> 00:20:18.030
It has to handle an
immense amount of traffic.

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:20.760
We're moving 10 terabytes here.

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:23.613
We can move a lot more
than that, actually.

00:20:23.613 --> 00:20:25.030
And it needs to
be cost efficient.

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:27.400
Your business needs to
be able to afford it.

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:29.770
And it needs to be
available all the time.

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:33.360
So to make this work, we
have a special network.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:35.640
And Subhasree is going
to talk about the network

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:39.590
and the software that
makes this possible.

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:41.720
Subhasree--

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:43.220
SUBHASREE MANDAL:
Thank you, Carter.

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:49.780
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:50.370
I'm Subhasree.

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:53.040
And I'm going to talk about
the network that supports

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:55.880
these kind of applications.

00:20:55.880 --> 00:21:00.480
So at Google, we believe network
is a key differentiator when

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:02.850
it comes to building
an application that's

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:05.650
scalable and available.

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:09.240
So Google has been investing
quite a bit in this technology

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:10.890
for over a decade.

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:13.680
The network infrastructure
team in Google

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:15.630
have been building
a unique network

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:18.930
infrastructure especially
driven by Google's needs.

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:20.940
Google's compute and
storage infrastructure

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:24.890
needed something that didn't
exist in the industry.

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:28.640
So we built unique and
innovative products.

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:33.770
And many of them have been
published as research papers.

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:37.380
And they have influenced
the industry, the academia.

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:42.030
But most importantly, they have
served Google's applications--

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:45.500
the essential applications
that have over a billion users,

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:48.530
like Search, Gmail, YouTube.

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.620
And going forward, the
same infrastructure

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:54.860
is going to be available
for our Google--

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:59.300
our cloud customers who can
build their products on that.

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:07.430
So this is a snapshot
of Google Cloud network.

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:10.850
The locations here
are cloud regions,

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:13.080
current and future ones.

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:17.390
And not all of them are
Cloud Bigtable regions yet.

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:19.790
The footprints keep growing.

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:23.450
And I would like us to focus
on the dense graph that

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:25.400
connects these locations.

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:27.800
There are plenty of
terrestrial links,

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:30.980
as well a lot of
trans-oceanic links.

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:33.240
And these trans-oceanic
links are quite interesting.

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:36.650
We will talk about those
a little more in details.

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:38.960
Some of these
trans-oceanic links Google

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:40.890
actually have invested in.

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:48.260
For example, you can see there
is one from Florida to Brazil.

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:50.660
It's called Monet
cable system that's

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:54.530
operated by Google along with
a few other telecom companies.

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:57.290
We'll talk more about those.

00:22:57.290 --> 00:22:59.330
So talking about the
density of the graph,

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:04.770
that reflects the scale and
availability of the network.

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:08.090
So the application, like the
one we are going to demonstrate,

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:10.610
is not running on
public internet.

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:13.370
It's trending on a
private global backbone

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:14.900
that's run by Google.

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:17.900
So every packet
traverses a router

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:20.040
that's controlled by Google.

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:22.100
And one of the
catastrophic failures

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:26.420
in a normal network is fiber
that causes partition and that

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:29.330
causes system
inconsistencies, et cetera--

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:32.670
are quite rare in
this kind of scenario.

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:34.550
So many of these
regions are actually

00:23:34.550 --> 00:23:37.580
hosted in our
massive data centers.

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:42.230
And each data center by rule has
at least three different fibers

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:45.110
that connect it to the
global network, which

00:23:45.110 --> 00:23:48.110
means fiber cut,
which is often treated

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:49.760
as a catastrophic
failure, is not

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:52.880
a catastrophic
failure in this case.

00:23:52.880 --> 00:24:01.150
And yet another example is the
trans-Atlantic connectivity.

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:04.240
As you can see, there are
six different fiber routes

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:07.330
that connect our
North American regions

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:09.610
to our European regions.

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:10.900
What does that mean?

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:13.210
That definitely
talks about scale.

00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:15.640
Each of these fibers can
carry several terabits

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:17.710
per second of traffic.

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:18.910
And there are six of them.

00:24:18.910 --> 00:24:22.460
And we expect them to
fail independently,

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:24.520
which means we can
provide stricter bandwidth

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:26.890
guarantee over these
links across the ocean.

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:33.540
And next, I'm going to show
you a little bit of the

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:36.670
behind the scenes story
how this is built.

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:39.690
So this is a cartoon
view of one of those six

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:45.480
trans-Atlantic cables that
connects Virginia to London.

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:48.240
And this is, of course,
not drawn to scale.

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:51.240
I have a sample
cable for you to see.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:54.630
You can come and take a
look after the presentation.

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:57.390
So it's probably not as
thick as you would imagine.

00:24:57.390 --> 00:25:00.850
I can wrap my fingers
easily around it.

00:25:00.850 --> 00:25:03.370
And at the center
of this cable, there

00:25:03.370 --> 00:25:07.930
is the optical fiber
that carries the signal

00:25:07.930 --> 00:25:10.142
as pulses of light.

00:25:10.142 --> 00:25:14.970
I also have a spool of
25 kilometers of fiber.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:17.890
Take a look at it,
if you want, later.

00:25:17.890 --> 00:25:20.100
So each of these
fibers, it's made

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:22.440
of glass-- very
high quality glass

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:25.890
with no impurities whatsoever.

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:30.270
And each of these
fiber strand, it's

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:32.790
approximately as thick
as a strand of hair.

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:38.350
But it can carry 100
terabits per second of data.

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:39.560
What does that mean?

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:41.790
100 terabits per
second would translate

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:47.430
to something like 10 million
streaming YouTube videos.

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:50.490
And as you can see, the
thickness of the cable

00:25:50.490 --> 00:25:53.250
gets thicker, gets
wider near the shore,

00:25:53.250 --> 00:25:57.010
because it's high
risk for damage.

00:25:57.010 --> 00:26:01.510
And these are laid
out using ships.

00:26:01.510 --> 00:26:03.180
You can see a cartoon
version of that.

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:06.210
Sometimes there are two
ships coming from two ends.

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:09.150
And in the middle of
the ocean, the cable

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:13.020
is lifted off the ocean floor,
and spliced, and buried back

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:15.120
into the ocean bed.

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:17.166
We'll see a video soon.

00:26:17.166 --> 00:26:19.890
And we lay out these paths.

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:22.740
We survey the path
earlier so that we

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:28.410
avoid rough terrains, and
coral reefs, hungry sharks--

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:29.850
so we'll see them in a bit.

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:37.970
So water is not the
first thing that

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:40.970
will come to mind when
you talk about networking.

00:26:40.970 --> 00:26:44.510
However, this is where a large
chunk of our network lives--

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:45.710
not in the data centers.

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:48.960
Data centers do have
a piece, though.

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:52.245
And even though we
don't think about water,

00:26:52.245 --> 00:26:57.740
but 2/3 of the Earth's
surface is covered with water,

00:26:57.740 --> 00:27:00.850
which means we cannot build
a network around the globe

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:03.220
without laying cables
under these water bodies.

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:08.670
The network is slow.

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:12.540
But people have been laying out
these cables for a long time,

00:27:12.540 --> 00:27:14.630
for over 150 years.

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:16.740
And these were not for
this kind of data network.

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:18.570
It was for telegraphy.

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:20.490
What has changed
more recently is

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:23.220
what goes inside these cables.

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:26.730
So we'll see in a bit how
these cables are constructed

00:27:26.730 --> 00:27:29.220
and how they are
laid out spatially

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:31.605
with the example of
Monet Cable System.

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:41.190
In the center of the cable,
we have the glass fiber.

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:43.310
These are typically color coded.

00:27:43.310 --> 00:27:45.240
Why?

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:48.960
If we want to repair, we want
to splice blue with blue and red

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:49.530
with red.

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:51.990
These are used for
identification.

00:27:51.990 --> 00:27:54.000
And these are
typically allocated

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:55.980
pairwise, one in
each direction--

00:27:55.980 --> 00:27:58.860
transmit and receive.

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:01.120
And of course, there are a
lot of protective layers.

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:03.660
There is one layer
that is very special.

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:05.310
That's the copper layer.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.320
Because it's not
just for protection.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:10.780
It's used for carrying
power to the repeaters that

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:13.320
sit along the path.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:15.570
And after, with
all these layers,

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:19.660
it's now ready to be
buried under the sea floor.

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:23.110
And if any of you happen to
visit the Sunnyvale office,

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:25.870
we have a model of
one of these boats.

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:26.970
Feel free to check it out.

00:28:31.863 --> 00:28:32.530
What about them?

00:28:35.873 --> 00:28:36.790
Maybe we trained them.

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:42.250
Actually, to speak
the truth, the sharks

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:44.270
do bite these cables
once in a while.

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:47.350
But with all the
protective layers,

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:50.330
they do not really damage the
cables or cause an outage.

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:52.000
So we cannot really blame them.

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:58.970
Now, back to how
this network is used.

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:02.960
So one key observation
is that there

00:29:02.960 --> 00:29:05.330
is a lot more traffic
between servers

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:08.810
than a server and an user.

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:12.570
And the inter-server traffic
is not only high in volume,

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:15.860
it also has very
different characteristics.

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:19.580
It's more bursty, there's
no smooth diurnal pattern,

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:23.600
and more amenable
to classification.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:25.580
There's so much of this
traffic that we built

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:27.440
a whole separate backbone.

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:28.430
We call it B4.

00:29:28.430 --> 00:29:32.510
That's our backend backbone
that connects our data centers.

00:29:32.510 --> 00:29:36.340
And people say
developers at Google

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:40.040
get to use wide area
network just like people

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:44.670
get to use local area network
in other places thanks to B4.

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:47.150
But this privilege should
not be limited just

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:49.040
to Google Developers.

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:51.980
With Cloud offerings
like Cloud Bigtable,

00:29:51.980 --> 00:29:55.280
this network would be available
to our cloud users, as well.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:01.555
So what does it take to
build a network like this?

00:30:01.555 --> 00:30:03.430
Scale is, of course, a
very challenging goal.

00:30:03.430 --> 00:30:06.640
And everything at Google
has to be built at scale.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:10.930
And in presence of scale, other
goals also become challenging.

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:15.430
One thing to note here is
that as our compute scales,

00:30:15.430 --> 00:30:18.940
network scales quadratically
because of its source dist

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:22.840
pairwise nature, which means
unit cost does not necessarily

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:26.390
go down, unlike
computer storage.

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:29.750
And availability, of
course, is our final goal.

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:33.950
Without availability,
bandwidth makes no sense.

00:30:33.950 --> 00:30:35.650
So I'll talk about
two approaches

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:38.140
that we take to build
a network like this.

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:39.460
One is a hardware approach.

00:30:39.460 --> 00:30:41.240
And one is a software approach.

00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:47.480
So in terms of
hardware, we do not

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:52.190
use lot of expensive
feature rich routers.

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:54.680
We use cheap commodity hardware.

00:30:54.680 --> 00:30:57.880
We mesh them and
build big switches.

00:30:57.880 --> 00:31:01.040
So Supergate is our
regional network

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:04.880
that we use in most
of our data centers

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:07.020
to connect zones
within the region

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:09.560
and pair with other regions.

00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:13.190
And it delivers 1.6
petabits per second

00:31:13.190 --> 00:31:15.770
of non-blocking capacity.

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:18.650
And 1.6 petabits
per second would

00:31:18.650 --> 00:31:22.890
translate to a few hundred
million streaming videos.

00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:25.610
And that's all within a region.

00:31:25.610 --> 00:31:27.730
And this picture shows
one of the eight building

00:31:27.730 --> 00:31:29.710
blocks of Supergate.

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:32.170
It's built with a few
hundred cheap commodity

00:31:32.170 --> 00:31:37.750
switches meshed to provide
2,000 ports, each with 100 gig

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:39.970
capacity.

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:42.465
So this is the hardware
piece of the solution.

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:48.180
And on the software
side, we follow

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:50.490
a philosophy called
software-defined networking

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:52.020
technology.

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:55.290
The main idea is to take the
smarts out of the network

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:58.110
and run them in servers
that are available.

00:31:58.110 --> 00:32:00.390
In traditional network
routing and control software

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:03.930
typically runs on the CPU
that lives inside the routers,

00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:05.470
which is limited.

00:32:05.470 --> 00:32:09.270
So for us, these get to run
on powerful Google servers.

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:12.810
And we get to leverage all of
Google's distributed computing

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:14.400
infrastructure.

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:19.080
As a result, we can run
advanced optimization algorithms

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:22.950
pretty fast and utilize
all the links that

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:26.100
have been deployed optimally.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:29.310
That means our network
is more efficient.

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:32.730
And also, because of the
good compute infrastructure,

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:35.280
the algorithms
converge faster, which

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:38.370
means if there is a fiber cut,
the network converges fast.

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:41.080
So that produces
high availability.

00:32:41.080 --> 00:32:44.420
It's much faster compared to
traditional MPLS/RSVP style

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:44.920
networks.

00:32:48.090 --> 00:32:51.870
So with all these,
what have we achieved?

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:55.520
So the graph on the left shows
how the scale of our network

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:58.580
has grown in recent
five year period.

00:32:58.580 --> 00:33:02.140
The traffic has grown by
two orders of magnitude.

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:06.470
And at the same time, actually
the last year of that period,

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:08.060
we have measured availability.

00:33:08.060 --> 00:33:11.132
And it has gone up
by at least one nine.

00:33:11.132 --> 00:33:14.480
One nine actually translates
to 10x improvement

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:17.270
in availability.

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:19.160
And hierarchical
control system based

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:23.820
on software-defined networking
allowed us to do this.

00:33:23.820 --> 00:33:27.220
With that, I am
going to wrap up.

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:28.640
We are not done yet.

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:32.360
The network continues to evolve,
because Google believes network

00:33:32.360 --> 00:33:33.740
is a key differentiator.

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:37.090
And we have to continue to
evolve and invest in it.

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:40.330
We are not only delivering
new features, as needed

00:33:40.330 --> 00:33:43.750
by our customers, which is
Google applications and cloud

00:33:43.750 --> 00:33:46.370
applications, we are
growing in scale.

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:50.260
We continue to innovate,
upgrade in place.

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:56.820
And we continue to improve scale
availability and decrease cost.

00:33:56.820 --> 00:33:59.970
With that, I'm going to
hand it over to Carter.

00:33:59.970 --> 00:34:01.563
Thank you.

00:34:01.563 --> 00:34:03.290
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:05.940
CARTER PAGE: Thanks,
thanks Subhasree.

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:10.250
1.6 petabits per second is
pretty bonkers, isn't it?

00:34:10.250 --> 00:34:13.250
All right, so we
learned about how

00:34:13.250 --> 00:34:15.679
the software defined
network and all this fiber

00:34:15.679 --> 00:34:18.170
that we own or share--

00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:20.150
some of it we own,
some of it we share--

00:34:20.150 --> 00:34:25.070
allows us to have the scale,
the cost effectiveness,

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:27.770
and the availability
to provide the data

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:31.219
locality around the world
for a solution like Bigtable.

00:34:31.219 --> 00:34:33.960
So what we're going to do now is
we're going to go back to Doug.

00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:35.960
All right, we're going
to pray to the demo gods.

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:38.659
And let's see how we're doing.

00:34:42.969 --> 00:34:44.820
DOUG MCERLEAN: So I'm smiling.

00:34:44.820 --> 00:34:48.900
The last one finished
10 seconds ago.

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:51.750
The other two have
been done for a bit.

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:55.065
So just to remind what we were
doing, we are running writes--

00:34:57.590 --> 00:34:58.450
yeah, there we go.

00:34:58.450 --> 00:35:01.290
We're running writes
through default routing.

00:35:01.290 --> 00:35:03.330
We're writing
victory into Athens.

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:07.200
And that's going to the
original cluster in Oregon.

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:09.990
And we have
replication set up now

00:35:09.990 --> 00:35:12.640
between these four
regions around the world.

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:15.650
So if we cut back to the--

00:35:15.650 --> 00:35:17.580
there-- so you can see
these are all done.

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:21.098
We're waiting on a local pass to
collect disk usage on this one,

00:35:21.098 --> 00:35:22.140
because it just finished.

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:24.330
But everything is all set up.

00:35:24.330 --> 00:35:25.810
Replication is running.

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:28.820
So the next thing
we're going to do

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:31.680
is we're going to throw
over to the monitoring page

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:37.240
to show that we've been
writing the whole time.

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:40.672
And then we're going
to kill these writes.

00:35:40.672 --> 00:35:41.880
They've served their purpose.

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:44.670
And we're going to see how
many times this write happened

00:35:44.670 --> 00:35:46.980
in the original cluster
so we can sort of check

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:49.140
for consistency here.

00:35:49.140 --> 00:35:52.950
So I'm going to run
this command here.

00:35:52.950 --> 00:35:56.015
This is, again, the
CBT command line tool.

00:35:56.015 --> 00:35:57.140
It's pointed at this table.

00:35:57.140 --> 00:36:00.700
It's doing a lookup now into
demo table for row Athens.

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:01.950
I'm going to grep for victory.

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:03.950
And I'm going to count
the lines that come back.

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:11.490
And we've got 3,115.

00:36:11.490 --> 00:36:13.530
So now what we want
to do is see if that's

00:36:13.530 --> 00:36:14.707
the number somewhere else.

00:36:14.707 --> 00:36:16.290
But like I said,
this default routing,

00:36:16.290 --> 00:36:18.870
if we don't tell where
to send the request,

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:20.670
it's going to go to
this original cluster.

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:23.220
So we need to set
some routing rules up.

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:26.370
To do that, I'm going to go
over here into the application

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:26.953
profiles page.

00:36:26.953 --> 00:36:29.370
This is a cool feature I'm
going to talk a little bit more

00:36:29.370 --> 00:36:30.390
in a second.

00:36:30.390 --> 00:36:31.890
But just to keep
things going, we're

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:33.098
going to create one of these.

00:36:33.098 --> 00:36:35.700
These let you
control your routing

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:36.910
at a fine grained level.

00:36:36.910 --> 00:36:40.220
And so I'm going to create
one called check copy,

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:42.000
give a little description.

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:45.240
And I'll point this at,
say, the Asia cluster.

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:47.330
So this is C4.

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:48.460
And I'll create it.

00:36:48.460 --> 00:36:51.480
And all this is is an ID that
you've set up with the server.

00:36:51.480 --> 00:36:53.630
And then when you send
this ID with a request,

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:57.560
we will route things as
you set up for that ID.

00:36:57.560 --> 00:36:58.970
So we've created
the new profile.

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:00.762
Here, you can see the
old one, the default.

00:37:00.762 --> 00:37:04.430
And that's routing at C1,
which is the original thing.

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:06.440
And now we just run
this command again.

00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:14.110
All we have to do is add app
profile equals check copy.

00:37:14.110 --> 00:37:19.820
Run it again, and we
should get 3,115 again.

00:37:19.820 --> 00:37:20.990
And there it is.

00:37:20.990 --> 00:37:23.600
So even though we
were writing before,

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:26.800
during, and after these
copies, replication

00:37:26.800 --> 00:37:28.450
has kept this data in sync.

00:37:28.450 --> 00:37:30.970
It took probably only about
two seconds for this to come

00:37:30.970 --> 00:37:35.240
across, which is sort of the
typical 99th percentile number

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:37.240
that we generally see for
these sorts of setups,

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:38.590
especially since there's
not a lot of traffic

00:37:38.590 --> 00:37:39.382
here to begin with.

00:37:41.700 --> 00:37:43.837
So replication did
the heavy lifting.

00:37:43.837 --> 00:37:46.170
I just want to talk about app
profiles for a little bit.

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:48.195
And then I'll show
a monitoring graph--

00:37:48.195 --> 00:37:49.800
it just takes a
little while to load--

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:53.370
that shows that this actually
routed where we said it did.

00:37:53.370 --> 00:37:55.380
So app profiles, like
I said, are an ID

00:37:55.380 --> 00:37:57.370
that you set up in advance.

00:37:57.370 --> 00:37:59.750
And I want to plug
these to people who

00:37:59.750 --> 00:38:01.080
are looking into replication.

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:02.580
I also want to plug
these for people

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:06.255
who are just using Bigtable and
not ready for replication yet.

00:38:06.255 --> 00:38:09.600
All an app profile is is a
way to slice your traffic.

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:11.760
We provide some config
on the server side

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:13.030
to let you control routing.

00:38:13.030 --> 00:38:16.680
But even if you don't
need that, it still

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:20.748
gives you per
application monitoring.

00:38:20.748 --> 00:38:22.290
So you can see, for
example, how much

00:38:22.290 --> 00:38:25.640
traffic is coming from your
batch job versus your streaming

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:31.140
ingestion versus your live
traffic serving reads.

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:35.730
And that's a useful
feature on its own.

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:39.670
In addition, if you setup
one of these per app,

00:38:39.670 --> 00:38:41.190
if you do want to
adopt replication,

00:38:41.190 --> 00:38:43.890
it gives you a fine grained
way to just move over

00:38:43.890 --> 00:38:46.050
one app at a time when
it's ready for replication.

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:50.080
And everything else will just
do what it was doing before.

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:51.910
And the best part
about that is you

00:38:51.910 --> 00:38:53.160
can just reroute it from here.

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:56.760
You don't have to down your
app or change any code.

00:38:56.760 --> 00:38:58.800
You just change the
server side config.

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:01.030
And we'll re-route
everything for you.

00:39:01.030 --> 00:39:03.515
So hopefully I
have convinced you.

00:39:03.515 --> 00:39:05.640
And hopefully I've also
talked long enough for this

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:07.960
to finish loading.

00:39:07.960 --> 00:39:12.590
So if we just go over
to monitoring again--

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:15.360
so we see one read here.

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:17.310
Looks like we did not
quite have enough time

00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:18.790
to see the other read come in.

00:39:18.790 --> 00:39:21.445
So I'll return to this later.

00:39:21.445 --> 00:39:23.070
I want to jump over
to another instance

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:25.920
now and show you some
disaster recovery.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:28.320
This is something that
Carter alluded to before.

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:31.930
And this is something that
replication makes very easy.

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:34.013
So if you can go
over to the slides,

00:39:34.013 --> 00:39:36.180
just show a nice diagram
of what we're going to do--

00:39:36.180 --> 00:39:38.340
go a couple forward.

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:41.850
So the new setup that
we have in this instance

00:39:41.850 --> 00:39:45.720
is we've got a primary serving
cluster in Iowa, US central.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:49.680
And we've got a backup
cluster in Oregon.

00:39:49.680 --> 00:39:52.710
And we've also got a load
generator running in Iowa.

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:56.370
And it's using failover,
or any cluster routing.

00:39:56.370 --> 00:39:57.840
This goes nearest first.

00:39:57.840 --> 00:40:01.500
So whichever cluster is close,
that's what we're going to use.

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:02.970
And we will only
try the other one

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:08.703
if the first request is slow
or errors out for some reason.

00:40:08.703 --> 00:40:10.620
And so you can see here
the traffic by default

00:40:10.620 --> 00:40:14.140
is all going to go right back
into Iowa where it started.

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:16.525
So if we cut back
to the demo, you

00:40:16.525 --> 00:40:18.400
can see that that's
exactly what's happening.

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:20.280
We've got these two clusters.

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:24.145
And right there you see that all
that 20,000 writes per second,

00:40:24.145 --> 00:40:25.770
they're all going to
the serve cluster.

00:40:25.770 --> 00:40:29.167
And none of them are going
to the backup right now.

00:40:29.167 --> 00:40:30.750
Another thing that
I want to point out

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:34.440
is that the backup is smaller.

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:36.748
The backup's not taking
any live traffic.

00:40:36.748 --> 00:40:38.790
And the replication load
that we're sending to it

00:40:38.790 --> 00:40:40.110
is a little bit more
efficient, because we

00:40:40.110 --> 00:40:42.750
can kind of batch and window
things behind the scenes.

00:40:42.750 --> 00:40:45.480
So even though it has one
third the compute power

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:48.717
of the serve cluster, it's
still able to keep up,

00:40:48.717 --> 00:40:49.800
which is all it has to do.

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:53.640
It's just trying to keep this
hot swappable copy of the data

00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:56.430
alive.

00:40:56.430 --> 00:40:59.940
So from there, we're going to go
back to the edit instance page.

00:40:59.940 --> 00:41:02.910
And we're going to simulate
what happens if that

00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:05.790
serve cluster goes down.

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:10.710
Imagine there's a lot of fiber
cuts, or there's a tornado--

00:41:10.710 --> 00:41:14.940
a big outage in the zone
that makes most or all

00:41:14.940 --> 00:41:17.400
of your requests start failing.

00:41:17.400 --> 00:41:18.900
I'm going to
simulate this by just

00:41:18.900 --> 00:41:21.277
deleting the serve cluster.

00:41:21.277 --> 00:41:22.860
And this is something
that I encourage

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:25.170
people to try themselves if
they're using replication

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:26.850
for this purpose,
because it lets

00:41:26.850 --> 00:41:29.370
you get a feel for
what you need to do

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:33.110
in the event of a
failover situation.

00:41:33.110 --> 00:41:34.110
So you can see already--

00:41:34.110 --> 00:41:36.360
I don't know if anybody was
looking at the CPU before.

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:40.060
The CPU is already going
up in the backup cluster.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:41.910
This is going to
update in a little bit

00:41:41.910 --> 00:41:42.870
the writes per second.

00:41:42.870 --> 00:41:46.140
But behind the scenes, what's
happening is everything

00:41:46.140 --> 00:41:47.160
is rerouting.

00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:49.652
It only takes a few
seconds for this to happen.

00:41:49.652 --> 00:41:52.110
It takes longer, unfortunately,
for the metrics to actually

00:41:52.110 --> 00:41:53.027
show up on the screen.

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:58.290
So imagine this did happen.

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.310
Let's actually just go
back to the slides so we

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:02.070
can get a nice picture of this.

00:42:02.070 --> 00:42:04.380
And go to the next one.

00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:07.380
This is going to route
everything to Oregon now.

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:11.733
If this actually happens, there
are a few things going on.

00:42:11.733 --> 00:42:13.150
In this case, the
cluster is gone.

00:42:13.150 --> 00:42:15.870
But if the cluster were still
there and throwing errors,

00:42:15.870 --> 00:42:17.995
every request is going to
take a little bit of time

00:42:17.995 --> 00:42:19.860
to realize something's
up and failover.

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:21.840
So that's going to add
a little bit of latency.

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:23.423
And the fact that
it has to go further

00:42:23.423 --> 00:42:26.050
is also going to add a
little bit of latency.

00:42:26.050 --> 00:42:29.190
But the requests are still
going to get served eventually.

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:33.270
So this is a graceful
degradation situation.

00:42:33.270 --> 00:42:35.470
A couple of things that you
want to deal with here--

00:42:35.470 --> 00:42:37.710
number one, you might
want to sort of cut out

00:42:37.710 --> 00:42:38.850
that failover latency.

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:42.580
So in the real world, the
other cluster's still there.

00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:43.468
It's throwing errors.

00:42:43.468 --> 00:42:44.760
You'd want to try to delete it.

00:42:44.760 --> 00:42:47.048
If the zone is really
completely inaccessible,

00:42:47.048 --> 00:42:48.090
you might not be able to.

00:42:48.090 --> 00:42:50.850
But it's a good practice
to try that out first.

00:42:50.850 --> 00:42:53.820
And then the next thing that
you're going to want to do

00:42:53.820 --> 00:42:56.550
is size up the backup cluster.

00:42:56.550 --> 00:42:58.970
Carter mentioned this
is very easy to do.

00:42:58.970 --> 00:43:01.330
All you have to do, again,
is click a couple buttons.

00:43:01.330 --> 00:43:03.150
And this is because we
provisioned it lower

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:04.233
for cost saving purposes.

00:43:04.233 --> 00:43:06.150
But now it's actually
taking the full traffic.

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:08.850
And so we're going to
want to make it bigger

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:11.070
so that it can handle the
throughput without falling

00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:11.670
behind.

00:43:11.670 --> 00:43:13.860
Requests will be
served a little slower,

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:16.230
but they won't knock
the cluster over.

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:17.280
And that's already done.

00:43:17.280 --> 00:43:18.780
In the next few
minutes, we're going

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:20.080
to start re-balancing the load.

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:23.550
And you're going to see the
CPU utilization go down.

00:43:23.550 --> 00:43:26.103
Now hopefully--
again, those graphs

00:43:26.103 --> 00:43:27.270
take a little while to load.

00:43:27.270 --> 00:43:30.420
But hopefully we can see
already the initial failover

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:31.400
from this going on.

00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:38.000
Yeah, so right here, you can
see traffic falling off a cliff

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:40.250
to the serve cluster
and traffic coming up

00:43:40.250 --> 00:43:42.530
to the backup cluster.

00:43:42.530 --> 00:43:44.390
It's not quite all the
way to where it was.

00:43:44.390 --> 00:43:47.510
And that's a consequence
of that extra latency.

00:43:47.510 --> 00:43:50.480
But the failover is working.

00:43:50.480 --> 00:43:51.902
And so once you've
sort of handled

00:43:51.902 --> 00:43:53.610
the immediate emergency,
things are still

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:55.500
in the degraded state.

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:57.620
We're going to do what
we did in the other demo.

00:43:57.620 --> 00:43:59.412
And we're just going
to add another cluster

00:43:59.412 --> 00:44:02.240
to set up replication
someplace else that's working.

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:03.910
So we can add a cluster.

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:06.080
Say, if it's just
a zonal outage,

00:44:06.080 --> 00:44:08.540
the original one was
in US central 1B.

00:44:08.540 --> 00:44:12.470
We can add a cluster
in US central 1C.

00:44:12.470 --> 00:44:15.380
And it will be
copied in the time

00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:17.631
that it takes to come up
and talk about networking.

00:44:20.385 --> 00:44:21.510
And that'll spin for a bit.

00:44:21.510 --> 00:44:23.070
And then it will be done.

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:25.860
So that's failover.

00:44:25.860 --> 00:44:29.000
I just want to check back
over here and see if I-- yup,

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:30.750
so this is the original
demo, just showing

00:44:30.750 --> 00:44:33.330
that that second read request
went to the other cluster.

00:44:36.500 --> 00:44:37.880
And that's it.

00:44:37.880 --> 00:44:39.547
I'm going to hand
things back to Carter.

00:44:41.790 --> 00:44:43.370
CARTER PAGE: All
right, thanks Doug.

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:47.600
So back to the slides here--
so just to wrap up, you get--

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:49.940
with the failover
solution, we saw

00:44:49.940 --> 00:44:52.580
that we were able to get fast
global access to a single data

00:44:52.580 --> 00:44:53.480
set.

00:44:53.480 --> 00:44:56.670
We copied terabytes of
data in just minutes.

00:44:56.670 --> 00:44:59.420
We do basically heavily
parallelized writes

00:44:59.420 --> 00:45:01.670
over this massive fiber network.

00:45:01.670 --> 00:45:04.310
So we're actually able
to copy a lot of data

00:45:04.310 --> 00:45:06.080
in a short amount of time.

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:08.660
And it's so fast,
as you noticed,

00:45:08.660 --> 00:45:11.070
one of the things we were
talking about is, hey,

00:45:11.070 --> 00:45:13.560
we need an extra cluster
in our instance--

00:45:13.560 --> 00:45:15.560
let's just spin up another
one on the other side

00:45:15.560 --> 00:45:18.980
of the continent and
just copy the data there.

00:45:18.980 --> 00:45:22.130
And that's actually something
that works pretty well.

00:45:22.130 --> 00:45:25.430
You get disaster
recovery out of the box.

00:45:25.430 --> 00:45:28.340
Scaling up, scaling
down takes seconds.

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:30.530
And you can re-route
individual applications.

00:45:30.530 --> 00:45:32.770
You can use
application profiles,

00:45:32.770 --> 00:45:34.520
configure each different
application using

00:45:34.520 --> 00:45:35.690
a different profile,
even if they're all

00:45:35.690 --> 00:45:37.130
using the same routing rules.

00:45:37.130 --> 00:45:39.350
That gives you a lot of
flexibility operationally

00:45:39.350 --> 00:45:43.590
until you decide what you
want to do with that traffic.

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:46.610
So in summary, with
global data replication

00:45:46.610 --> 00:45:48.510
you get a global presence.

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:51.170
So you're basically a global
local presence, essentially.

00:45:51.170 --> 00:45:54.100
You get the low
latency of being local.

00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:57.440
But that data is kept in
sync around the entire world

00:45:57.440 --> 00:46:00.440
in seconds, typically, and
in minutes on the long tail.

00:46:00.440 --> 00:46:02.720
You have single digit
milliseconds latency,

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:03.870
as I mentioned.

00:46:03.870 --> 00:46:06.120
It's a very high scale system.

00:46:06.120 --> 00:46:10.070
We haven't found anyone who can
push it beyond its limits yet.

00:46:10.070 --> 00:46:13.400
If you think you can,
we want to talk to you.

00:46:13.400 --> 00:46:15.410
And we have an out
of the box disaster

00:46:15.410 --> 00:46:17.360
recovery solution
with this, as well.

00:46:17.360 --> 00:46:20.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

